{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And when a man schemes against his fellow to kill him.</b> Its explanation: והרגו (and he kills him). And Scripture needed to use the language of cunning (בערמה) in the law of murder, because everyone who murders his fellow deliberately employs cunning in the matter, seeking an hour that is convenient for him to do so with his own hands; and further, because he draws force from the cunning serpent (הנחש הערום) who caused Ḥavah to sin, from whom was born Qayin, the first murderer — and therefore the Sages call everyone who employs cunning in sin a רשע ערום (a cunning wicked man), which is what they always say: איזהו רשע ערום? And he is called in the language of the Torah a רשע רע, as is said (Isaiah 3:11): ״אוי לרשע רע״ — because he draws force from that force which is called רע (evil), which is the soul of the sphere of Mars (מאדים), from which evil opens upon all the inhabitants of the earth; and likewise the star Mars is called רעה in the language of Scripture. And I have already mentioned this in the section of בא אל פרעה." "<b>From My altar you shall take him.</b> Scripture says: it goes without saying if he fled to one of the cities of refuge mentioned above, ושמתי לך מקום — that they take him out from there. But even if he went and seized the horns of the altar like Yoav, of whom it is written (I Kings 2:28): ״וינס יואב אל אהל ה׳ ויחזק בקרנות המזבח״ — they take him out from there and put him to death. And this is truly among the laws of equity and included among righteous statutes and judgments: that even this one who thought to be helped by the Holy One, blessed be He, or by the altar attributed to Him — since he is a murderer and has incurred the death penalty, Scripture commanded to take him out from there and deliver him into the hand of his adversary; and it is not fitting to show pity and compassion upon him, for compassion in this place is cruelty toward the creatures." "<b>And Rashi</b>, of blessed memory, explained: מעם מזבחי — even a priest engaged in the divine service; and this is a midrash of our Sages, of blessed memory. And as for Yoav's seizing the horns of the altar, our Sages, of blessed memory, already said in tractate Makkot, chapter אלו הן הגולין: that he erred, for he thought that its horns grant asylum, but it grants asylum only by its roof, as it is said מעם מזבחי and not מעל מזבחי — and he grasped its horns. Or he thought that Scripture speaks only of one who kills with witnesses and warning and by the court's decree, and this is the meaning of תקחנו למות — and they do not execute except with witnesses and warning; but for one put to death by royal authority, he thought it would grant asylum." "<b>And one may</b> say: מעם מזבחי — that it refers to the witness, to say that if the witness were standing and offering upon the altar, from there תקחנו למות — you shall take him to put him to death, to testify against him. And we may learn from here that the destruction of the wicked and their cutting off from the world and executing justice upon them is chosen for HaShem over the altar, as it is said (Psalms 50:23): ״וזבח תודה יכבדנני ושם דרך אראנו בישע אלהים״ — and it says (Proverbs 21:3): ״עשות משפט וצדקה נבחר לה׳ מזבח״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {3 "His closing prooftext chain is introduced by וכן אומר וזה שאמר הכתוב (וזה) — the first citation is from Psalms 50:23 and the second from Proverbs 21:3; rendered as they appear."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:35.282885520Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}